{
  "abstract": "For the shortcut Collatz map, a visit below the starting integer and a meeting with the orbit of a prescribed smaller integer both furnish induction certificates, but their clock requirements can differ substantially. We construct a dyadic class on which every start descends at the eighth step and a specified smaller affine partner has no uniform meeting deadline, even when the two trajectories are allowed different elapsed times. Given any finite deadline and any number of fixed low bits of a reference start, an explicit arithmetic progression of positive extensions avoids every meeting within both clock budgets. The reference pair itself coalesces. The proof separates equality of reference states from equality of the perturbation slopes, and gives a finite criterion for deciding whether a prescribed pair has a bounded meeting certificate near a given start. We compare this obstruction with a congruence construction that excludes all smaller visitors during a bounded initial climb, and with simultaneous finite excursions of consecutive starts obtained from negative trajectories. Exact examples exhibit positive certificates from the same affine partner, shorter routes through alternative partners, and arbitrarily long finite strings of increasing role exchanges. A moving-sum experiment and a unary theta test quantify two ways of obscuring existing descent information. All main numerical fixtures use a standalone exact-integer verifier. The results concern finite certificates and do not establish universal Collatz convergence.",
